# Brain Assistant project rules

## Repo map

- `frontend/` — Angular 19 PWA, standalone components, NGXS state.
- `backend/` — Express + Mongoose API, ESM, use-case architecture.
- `contracts/` — `@brainassistant/contracts`, shared API types linked into both apps as `file:../contracts`.
- `docs/design/` — product visual system: tokens, UI style guide, approved screen specs.
- `db/`, `nginx/`, `scripts/`, `docker-compose.yml` — infrastructure.

`frontend`, `backend` and `contracts` are separate npm packages. Run `npm`/`ng` commands from inside the relevant folder, not from the repo root.

Each of the three has an `AGENTS.md` describing its layout, the canonical code to imitate for each kind of task, and the code that must not be copied. Read the one for the package you are touching before writing code in it.

## Shell

The dev environment is Windows PowerShell. Do not chain commands with `&&` and do not use bash-only syntax; use `;` or separate commands.

## Shared contracts

- API request/response types belong in `contracts/`, not duplicated per app.
- After editing `contracts/`, it must be rebuilt before either app sees the change: `cd contracts; npm run build`.
- `frontend/src/app/shared/dto/` still holds legacy duplicated auth DTOs. Do not add new DTOs there — extend `contracts/` instead.
- The frontend is an offline-capable PWA with a sync protocol, so users keep running cached builds for a long time. Treat contracts as a public API: prefer additive, backward-compatible changes, and state explicitly when a change is breaking.
- In backend controllers, keep explicit `FooContract.Request` / `FooContract.Response` on the HTTP boundary (`ok()`, request mapping) even when inference would work. Do not strip them as redundant. See `.cursor/rules/backend-architecture.mdc`.

## `type` vs `interface`

House style, not the TypeScript handbook: `type` is an object **shape** (DTO, JSON, HTTP/Passport payload, test fixture). `interface` is a **contract** a class `implements`. A `type` is erased at compile time; it is not a class and does not imply instances. Do not convert `type` → `interface` for “consistency”. See `.cursor/rules/typescript-type-vs-interface.mdc`.

## UI Design

All frontend UI must follow `docs/design/UI_STYLE_GUIDE.md`.

All visual values must come from `docs/design/design-system.json`.

Do not invent colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows, icon styles,
or other visual conventions during feature implementation.

Reuse existing UI components and patterns before creating new ones.

If the existing design system cannot represent a required UI state,
propose an extension to the design system instead of silently introducing
a new visual convention.

Approved screen composition lives in `docs/design/screens/<name>.json`.
Do not put screen-specific colors or radii there, and do not create a
screen JSON until that screen’s design is approved.

Approved mockups live in `docs/design/references/`. PNG is a visual
reference (composition, density, proportions), not a source of tokens.
Do not measure colors, spacing, or radii from it. Commit only approved
images: one primary PNG per screen, plus extra PNGs only for important
interactive states.

## Verify, do not invent

Never invent API endpoints, database fields, env/config variable names, contract types, or existing behaviour. Read the relevant file first and reuse what is there.

Before adding a service, helper, mapper, model or component, search for an existing one. Both apps already have `shared/` layers for each of these.

## Git

Never create a git commit unless the user explicitly asked to commit in that message. Finishing an implementation stage, “go to the next step”, or having a clean working tree is not permission to commit.

If they asked to commit existing work and also to start new work, commit only what they named (or the already-unstaged changes they pointed at). Leave the new work uncommitted until they say to commit it.

## Scope of changes

- Keep the change scoped to what was asked: no drive-by renames, unrelated refactors, or reformatting of files you did not need to touch.
- Prefer modifying existing code over adding a parallel implementation beside it.
- Do not add dependencies without a clear reason; both apps already carry a large dependency set.
- Formatting and code style are handled by ESLint, Prettier, Stylelint and husky/lint-staged. Do not hand-format code.

## Before finishing

Check that the app you touched still compiles and that imports resolve.

If you changed observable behaviour and no existing spec would fail, add a test at the right layer:

- backend HTTP flow → integration spec in `backend/test/integration/`
- backend domain / mapper → unit spec in `backend/test/unit/`
- NGXS slice → `frontend/tests/unit/state/`
- pure helper, pipe, or frontend service → `frontend/tests/unit/`
- component template (title, button, `@if`, `data-test`) → `frontend/tests/unit/components/`
- user-visible flow → Playwright in `frontend/integration/`

Do not add mocked unit tests for backend use-cases or controllers.
Do not use `frontend/e2e/` for routine coverage.
Do not assert CSS classes, spacing, or `should create`.
